In earlier Android development, especially in Java-based apps, we commonly used classic design patterns like MVC, Singleton, Factory, Observer, and Adapter.

✳️ MVC was a go-to pattern, where Activity or Fragment acted as both View and Controller, and we had a Model class handling data. But this led to the issue of fat Activities, so we later started migrating toward MVP and eventually MVVM with Jetpack components.

🔁 Singleton was widely used for managing global instances like Retrofit clients, SharedPreferences, or SQLite helper. I always ensured it was thread-safe using either synchronized or double-checked locking with volatile.

🏭 For creating dynamic instances, like Fragments based on user type or status, I used the Factory Pattern to encapsulate object creation and keep code modular.

👂 I also used the Observer pattern a lot, especially for UI updates via interface callbacks or BroadcastReceivers—before LiveData and reactive frameworks were common.

🧩 And of course, the Adapter pattern is essential in Android—used heavily for ListView, Spinner, and now RecyclerView—to bind data models to the UI layer.

I appreciate these patterns for teaching the fundamentals of decoupled architecture, and I’ve transitioned many legacy Java codebases to modern architectures while still respecting their roots."

